WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.854 --> 00:00:04.743
A street with a distribution substation, a cable to every home,

2
00:00:04.975 --> 00:00:08.005
and a cold store at the end: all on the same grid.

3
00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:11.573
Grid congestion means that at peak moments,

4
00:00:11.816 --> 00:00:16.205
the demand for transport is higher than what part of the grid can safely carry.

5
00:00:17.207 --> 00:00:21.061
That part can be a cable, a transformer or a whole substation.

6
00:00:22.074 --> 00:00:27.449
It can be on the consumption side, like an industrial area, or on the feed-in side,

7
00:00:27.647 --> 00:00:28.901
like solar parks.

8
00:00:29.614 --> 00:00:32.818
Large parts of the Netherlands are full for one or both.

9
00:00:34.127 --> 00:00:39.154
This cold store is a large consumer: a connection above 3 times 80 amps,

10
00:00:39.270 --> 00:00:41.801
about 55 kilovolt amperes.

11
00:00:42.515 --> 00:00:45.696
So is a supermarket, a factory or a charging hub.

12
00:00:46.751 --> 00:00:51.755
In a congested area, new large consumers, and existing ones that want more,

13
00:00:51.894 --> 00:00:53.217
go on a waiting list.

14
00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:55.996
Thousands of companies are waiting.

15
00:00:56.992 --> 00:00:59.918
Homes and small shops had capacity set aside.

16
00:01:00.669 --> 00:01:07.333
Since July 2026 they follow the same priority rules, so sometimes they wait too.

17
00:01:08.317 --> 00:01:11.649
The public capacity map shows where there is still room,

18
00:01:11.765 --> 00:01:13.657
for consumption and for feed-in.

19
00:01:14.957 --> 00:01:16.060
Why not build more?

20
00:01:16.817 --> 00:01:22.413
A new substation often takes 5 to 10 years: permits, land, space for cables.

21
00:01:23.185 --> 00:01:26.564
Reinforcing medium voltage takes months to years.

22
00:01:27.610 --> 00:01:31.523
Technicians, transformers and space underground are scarce.

23
00:01:32.330 --> 00:01:37.346
Building alone will not solve it for now: the existing grid has to be used better.

24
00:01:38.342 --> 00:01:43.323
An example: a 30 megavolt ampere substation, with a peak of 29.

25
00:01:44.078 --> 00:01:47.375
Three companies together want 6 megavolt amperes more.

26
00:01:48.376 --> 00:01:49.537
That does not fit.

27
00:01:50.276 --> 00:01:56.046
The first answer: a waiting list, until a new substation is ready in about 7 years.

28
00:01:57.339 --> 00:02:03.608
But the grid operator looks closer: all 8,760 hours of the year in a row,

29
00:02:03.759 --> 00:02:04.757
from high to low.

30
00:02:05.734 --> 00:02:11.214
The load is above 24 megavolt amperes for only 180 hours a year.

31
00:02:11.965 --> 00:02:14.217
That is about 2 percent of the time.

32
00:02:15.191 --> 00:02:16.027
That is typical:

33
00:02:16.167 --> 00:02:21.275
a full station is usually only overloaded for tens to a few hundred hours a year.

34
00:02:22.564 --> 00:02:26.802
Congestion management means paying or contracting customers to use less,

35
00:02:26.976 --> 00:02:29.310
or feed in less, at peak moments.

36
00:02:30.289 --> 00:02:34.957
Grid operators often buy that flexibility through GOPACS, their joint platform,

37
00:02:35.084 --> 00:02:36.686
where companies offer to shift.

38
00:02:37.741 --> 00:02:40.632
Here the cold store shifts 4 megavolt amperes

39
00:02:40.667 --> 00:02:43.012
of cooling out of those hours for a fee,

40
00:02:43.187 --> 00:02:45.254
for example by cooling earlier.

41
00:02:46.273 --> 00:02:50.197
A flexible contract gives capacity except at peak hours,

42
00:02:50.336 --> 00:02:53.820
or for a set share of the year, usually at a lower tariff.

43
00:02:54.804 --> 00:02:56.975
One company takes such a contract.

44
00:02:57.724 --> 00:03:02.519
So two of the three connect within a year: a little flexibility frees a lot of room.

45
00:03:03.807 --> 00:03:05.769
Who gets scarce capacity first?

46
00:03:06.511 --> 00:03:08.636
By default: whoever comes first.

47
00:03:09.664 --> 00:03:13.809
Since 2024 the regulator has let grid operators

48
00:03:13.867 --> 00:03:16.247
put projects with social value first:

49
00:03:16.421 --> 00:03:17.942
congestion relief first,

50
00:03:18.139 --> 00:03:23.677
then safety, such as hospitals, then basic needs, such as new housing and schools.

51
00:03:24.378 --> 00:03:30.566
A court annulled that first framework in 2025; the new one keeps the same order.

52
00:03:31.571 --> 00:03:34.961
Companies further down the queue wait longer as a result.

53
00:03:36.001 --> 00:03:40.715
A sharp question: for how many hours is this station really over its limit,

54
00:03:40.866 --> 00:03:41.922
and by how much?

55
00:03:43.229 --> 00:03:44.135
Who runs this?

56
00:03:44.819 --> 00:03:47.907
The regional grid operator: it connects customers,

57
00:03:48.081 --> 00:03:51.982
transports electricity and often gas, keeps the grid safe,

58
00:03:52.214 --> 00:03:56.371
restores outages, day and night, and builds the grid of the future.

59
00:03:57.423 --> 00:04:01.405
Every two years it publishes an investment plan: its bottlenecks,

60
00:04:01.591 --> 00:04:02.752
and what it will build.

61
00:04:03.847 --> 00:04:05.438
It does not sell energy.

62
00:04:06.136 --> 00:04:10.687
An energy supplier does, such as Eneco or Vattenfall, and you choose your own.

63
00:04:12.027 --> 00:04:16.787
Most outages are in the medium and low voltage grid: a cable hit by digging,

64
00:04:16.973 --> 00:04:19.005
an old joint, a blown fuse.

65
00:04:20.018 --> 00:04:23.246
Operators track the outage minutes per customer per year.

66
00:04:23.965 --> 00:04:29.236
In 2025 that was about 23 minutes from unplanned outages,

67
00:04:29.387 --> 00:04:32.139
plus about 27 minutes of planned work.

68
00:04:32.892 --> 00:04:35.411
One of the most reliable grids in Europe.

69
00:04:36.449 --> 00:04:41.766
Example: 2.8 million customers and 70 million customer minutes in a year,

70
00:04:41.952 --> 00:04:44.657
mostly from medium-voltage cable faults.

71
00:04:45.449 --> 00:04:47.852
That is 25 minutes per customer.

72
00:04:48.866 --> 00:04:52.245
One fault that cuts 3,000 customers for 90

73
00:04:52.315 --> 00:04:56.378
minutes is already 270,000 customer minutes.

74
00:04:57.356 --> 00:04:59.632
So switch most customers back first,

75
00:04:59.969 --> 00:05:05.031
for example through the normally open point in the ring, and repair the cable after.

76
00:05:06.341 --> 00:05:10.521
A grid operator is a regulated monopoly: each has its own area,

77
00:05:10.741 --> 00:05:12.459
and customers cannot choose.

78
00:05:13.466 --> 00:05:17.495
So the ACM, the regulator, sets its income through the tariffs,

79
00:05:17.623 --> 00:05:20.780
fixes the rules in the codes, and checks quality.

80
00:05:21.748 --> 00:05:30.189
Since 1 January 2026 the Energy Act sets the framework, replacing the act of 1998.

81
00:05:30.921 --> 00:05:35.809
Provinces and municipalities own the operators, and selling them is not allowed.

82
00:05:36.585 --> 00:05:40.451
The state puts in capital, as in Stedin, and owns TenneT.

83
00:05:41.211 --> 00:05:43.939
Tariffs and waiting lists are national news.

84
00:05:45.025 --> 00:05:49.901
Meanwhile the work grows, and the sector is short of thousands of technicians,

85
00:05:50.122 --> 00:05:53.361
so operators standardise and plan bigger packages.

86
00:05:54.067 --> 00:05:57.701
The sharp question: where does a technician’s day actually go?
